# A variant or union ARM declares the TYPE of what it carries. A string literal
# is a value, not a type, so it names none and leaves the arm with none.
#
# Nothing reported that. Constructing the arm said "expected ?, got StringView",
# blaming the argument for a type the declaration never produced -- and merely
# MATCHING on the sum, which touches no arm payload at all, emitted C naming an
# undeclared struct while zc reported nothing and exited 0.
#
# Only the offending arm is skipped, not the whole definition: aborting would
# leave every later arm unresolved and report each use of one as a missing
# member, burying the real mistake under its own fallout. `two` below still
# resolves and still constructs, so this program fails once, for the reason it
# should.
#
# Sibling of `string_field_default.z` and worded apart from it on purpose: a
# field declared by value carries a DEFAULT, while an arm declares a payload
# type and has no default to carry.
v: variant {
    one: "x"
    two: i64
}

main: function is {
    a: v.two 5
    print "made"
}
